  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nA dreary Sunday continues across eastern Idaho, with afternoon \nsatellite imagery showing a broad cloud shield streaming \nnorthward across the Great Basin. Regional radar shows light-to-\nmoderate rainfall persisting across the area, though high- \nresolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) indicate \nprecipitation will steadily wind down around midnight. For the \nhigher terrain around Sun Valley, a Winter Weather Advisory \nremains in effect for elevations above 7,000 feet, but CAMs \nindicate snowfall will taper off by early this evening as \ndynamic lift weakens and moisture pushes further east. \n\nThe primary operational concern overnight will shift to low \nstratus and fog development given abundant low-level moisture \nover saturated soils. Surface winds are forecast to remain \nelevated across the Magic Valley, which should promote enough \nmixing to keep fog largely at bay there. However, lighter winds \nacross the Snake Plain\u2014especially the Upper Snake\u2014will allow the\nboundary layer to decouple and promote patchy fog development. \nPatchy fog has been introduced into the forecast grids to \naddress this impact through early Monday morning.\n\nCool, fall-like conditions will linger to start the workweek. As\nthe departing disturbance pulls eastward, cloud cover will\ngradually clear from west to east throughout the daytime hours\non Monday. Persistent cool advection and early morning cloud\ncover will suppress diurnal heating, keeping daytime highs\ncapped in the 40s to lower 50s across the region. Not much\nvariation from one day to the next through the upcoming workweek\nas far as temps are concerned. No major, high-impact storm\nsystems are on the horizon once today's precipitation clears\nout. Operational models depict a series of weak, transitory\nupper-level impulses tracking across the northern tier, which\nmay spark low-end PoPs occasionally across the higher terrain,\nbut lower-elevation valleys look predominantly dry. Medium-range\nensembles continue to trend toward building heights and above-\nnormal temperatures late in the period and into the start of\nnext week. Given typical model spread at days 6 and 7, this\nwarming trend will be monitored in subsequent cycles as guidance\nconverges.",
